cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  The Polak–Schrijver code has exactly eight private pairs (with the shannon
  repository, Stages 0–2W)
message: "If you use these materials, please cite the Zenodo record."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Artem
    family-names: Oktiabrev
    email: aoktyabrev@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-3626-2002"
abstract: >-
  Lower bounds on the Shannon capacity of C7. Every improvement since July 2026
  is built on one five-dimensional gadget: the 367-word Polak–Schrijver code with
  eight private pairs. Proved here: in the whole of Z_7^5 exactly eight vertices
  have a single neighbour in that code, they are precisely the eight in use, and
  their confusability graph is a matching with three edges, hence bipartite — so
  t* = 8 and there is no ninth private pair. Reported alongside: all eight
  367-word codes the Polak–Schrijver construction can produce, with private-pair
  counts 5, 6, 6, 6, 7, 7, 7, 8; the arithmetic that closes smaller codes; and a
  sweep of 8,260,976,640 images under Aut(C_7^5) in which the forbidden
  intersection is never empty, which is why the published construction has to
  replace one auxiliary vector. Two weaker observations are labelled as search
  evidence rather than proof. An exact verifier calibrated on cases where it can
  fail, a preregistration sealed before the first search run, a generated report,
  171 machine-checked verbatim quotations, and a script that reconciles every
  number in the note with the computation behind it.
keywords:
  - Shannon capacity
  - zero-error capacity
